---
description: Rules for AI-assisted development — always apply
alwaysApply: true
---

## Always

1. Follow Clean Architecture + MVVM/MVI (Intent / State / Effect / ViewModel / UseCase / Repository)
2. XML + View Binding only — never introduce Compose
3. Use the project's DI framework — register new bindings in the composition root
4. Use Version Catalog — no hardcoded dependency versions
5. Respect module boundaries — presentation must not depend on `:data`
6. Put user-facing text in string resources — no hardcoded UI strings
7. Match existing naming conventions (resources, packages, classes)
8. Add unit tests for new UseCases and ViewModels when adding features

## Never

1. Never add libraries without explicit human approval
2. Never add Hilt/Dagger/Compose/RxJava unless project already uses them
3. Never put business logic in Fragments/Activities beyond render + intent dispatch
4. Never access DataSources directly from presentation
6. Never skip `android:exported` on manifest components with intent-filters
7. Never use `GlobalScope` for feature work
8. Never modify unrelated files when implementing a feature

## New feature checklist

- [ ] Feature package: `{di,intent,state,effect,viewModel,ui}`
- [ ] Layout: `fragment_<feature>.xml` with prefixed view IDs
- [ ] Domain UseCase(s) + repository interface if new capability needed
- [ ] Data repository impl + DataSource if needed
- [ ] DI registration in composition root
- [ ] Strings in shared UI module (+ translations if project localizes)
- [ ] Unit tests for UseCase / ViewModel
- [ ] Verify no `:presentation` → `:data` dependency
